  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nWe are to find a step function h: [0,2] <- [0,1] that minimizes C5 = max_k int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx, subject to int_0^2 h(x) dx = 1. \nThe previous program achieved C5 ~ 0.380891. We can improve this by using a multi-resolution search combined with coordinate ascent and Bayesian optimization. \nKey bottlenecks in previous program: (1) Limited exploration space, (2) Suboptimal update methods, (3) Fixed resolution. Our approach employs several strategies to overcome these:\n1. Multi-resolution optimization: Start from coarse to fine searches\n2. Adaptive coordinate descent with step-size optimization\n3. Symmetric function representation to reduce search space\n4. Bayesian optimization to globally tune critical parameters\n\nMathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h_ninept: Piecewise-constant symmetric function on [0,2] defined by 9 break points (259 samples)\n- f: Optimization variable h in [0,1]^259\n- L = 2 (domain length), dx = 2/259\n- Integral constraint: sum(f) * dx = 1\n- Objective: C5 = (1/dx) * max_0^L \u27e8h, 1-h \u27e9_{L^2} (mode='full')\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Symmetric representation:\n   - Use only 9 significant break points (f has 259 points)\n   - Enforce h(2-x) = 1 - h(x) to balance overlap minimization\n\n2. Objective calculation:\n   - Build full_h (400 samples) from 9-parameter representation\n   - Compute correlation c = correlate(full_h, 1-full_h, mode='full')\n   - Return max(c)*dx\n\n3. Core optimization alternates between:\n   a) Coordinate descent phases with adaptive projection\n   b) Bayesian optimization phases over hyperparameters\n\n4. Multi-resolution components:\n   - resolution_levels = [10, 25, 40, ... up to 259 samples]\n   - Closure between levels via projection/interpolation\n\n5. Coordination between resolutions:\n   - Use matrix-free Anderson mixing\n   - Implement lagged-Fisher scoring for improved convergence\n\n6. Stopping criteria:\n   - Time budget: 1300 seconds\n   - Early stopping if objective plateaus\n   - Maintain multiple concurrent resolutions\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n1. Multi-resolution schedule (Worth trying at each budget level)\n   - Start at coarsest level to escape local minima\n   - Refine based on Bayesian improvement estimates\n\n2. Update strategy (Default):\n   - adaptive_coordinate_descent: Modeled after BFGS but with linear projections\n   - bayesian_optimizer: Local Bayesian optimization with warping kernel\n\n3. Parameter scheduling:\n   - Starting resolution: 10 samples\n   - Max resolution: 259 samples\n   - Project using cubic splines\n\nComputational budget and robustness\n1. Estimated wall-clock time breakdown:\n   - Resolution sweep: 20%\n   - Optimization phases: 60%\n   - Bayesian tuning: 20%\n\n2. Deadline monitoring:\n   - Each phase has budget ceiling (e.g., 500s for most expensive operation)\n   - Phased exit if improvement < 1e-4\n\n3. Recovery from failures:\n   - Maintain historical best solution\n   - Implement restart from last saved checkpoint\n\n4. Time-honored check for resolution change:\n   - New resolution = min(max(old_resolution * schedule_factor, min_resolution), max_resolution)\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n   - sum(f) * dx = 1\n\n2. Output format:\n   - 3-tuple: (h_values, C5, n_points)\n\n3. Objective re-evaluation:\n   - Use built-in correlation implementation for final check\n\n4. Validation sanity checks:\n   - Integral matching\n   - Objective monotonicity\n   - Symmetry enforcement",
